# =============================================================================
# Security headers -- apply to every response this file governs (the SPA
# shell + all static assets below). Kept as one /* block so there's a single
# source of truth; the tiered Cache-Control rules further down only ever set
# Cache-Control/Content-Type, so there's no header-name collision with this
# block regardless of Cloudflare's more-specific-path-wins merge order.
#
# NOT covered by this file: Pages Functions (/api/*, /tiles/*) -- Cloudflare
# does not apply _headers rules to Function-generated responses (same caveat
# already noted below for Cache-Control). Their equivalent security headers
# are set directly in functions/_shared/handlers.js and tileProxy.js instead.
#
# CSP is enforced (was Report-Only). This app is a WebGL map (MapLibre GL)
# pulling vector tiles/glyphs from OpenFreeMap and POSTing feedback to a
# Google Form -- satellite + elevation/DEM tiles are both proxied
# same-origin via functions/tiles/, so neither needs its own CSP allowance.
# Verified two ways before flipping from Report-Only: (1) every fetch(),
# external link, worker/blob use, and data:/inline-style/inline-script
# pattern in the codebase was traced against each directive below -- no
# mismatches; (2) a full production build was served locally with this
# exact header enforced (not Report-Only) and driven headlessly through
# app load + every bottom-nav tab, capturing the console the whole time --
# zero "Content-Security-Policy"/"Refused to" violation lines, and the
# handful of unrelated errors (OpenFreeMap host unreachable, some
# maplibre-gl image-load warnings) reproduced identically against the same
# build with NO Content-Security-Policy header at all, confirming they're
# sandbox-network artifacts, not CSP blocks. That headless pass couldn't
# reach the real OpenFreeMap/ArcGIS/S3 origins the live site depends on
# (sandboxed egress), so it's still worth one quick live sanity check --
# open the deployed site with DevTools' Console open, play a round in each
# mode including satellite, submit a Daily score, share, submit feedback --
# but nothing above suggests a directive is missing or wrong. Any future
# violation line names the exact directive to widen.
/*
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  Permissions-Policy: geolocation=(), camera=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()
  Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=31536000; includeSubDomains
  Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self' 'sha256-rCX7JYGzB59mQYNSxutx8zH/9EPcBLpfV0qbAg9GITU='; style-src 'self' 'unsafe-inline'; img-src 'self' data: blob: https://tiles.openfreemap.org; connect-src 'self' https://tiles.openfreemap.org https://docs.google.com; worker-src 'self' blob:; font-src 'self'; manifest-src 'self'; base-uri 'self'; form-action 'self'; object-src 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'

# Cloudflare Pages' default for every static asset is
# `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` -- the browser caches
# the body but re-checks with the origin (via ETag/If-None-Match) on every
# single request. That still saves bytes on a 304, but not the round-trip
# latency, which is what costs a returning player real time on a slow
# connection. Rules below add real cache lifetime, split into two risk
# tiers depending on whether a file's URL changes when its content does.
#
# NOTE: Pages Functions (/api/*, /tiles/*) ignore this file entirely --
# Cloudflare does not apply _headers rules to Function-generated responses,
# so nothing here can accidentally cache the leaderboard API or the
# satellite tile proxy (both already NetworkOnly at the service-worker
# layer in vite.config.js).

# --- Tier 1: content-hashed, safe to cache forever -------------------------
# Vite bakes a hash of each file's own contents into its filename, so any
# code or dependency change produces a brand-new URL. There's no such thing
# as a stale hit here -- `immutable` additionally skips revalidation even on
# a manual page reload, which plain max-age does not.
/assets/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

# --- Tier 2: unhashed files that rarely change -----------------------------
# Brand/icon assets only change during a deliberate icon-regeneration pass
# (a full redeploy each time). A day-long cache keeps repeat app opens
# instant without risking a long-lived stale icon if one is ever swapped.
/icons/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

/favicon.ico
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# Social-preview share card -- same regenerate-only-on-a-deliberate-pass
# profile as /icons/*, so it gets the same tier. Previously uncovered by
# any rule here, so it was silently falling through to Cloudflare Pages'
# default (`max-age=0, must-revalidate`) -- every crawler/chat-app fetch
# was paying a revalidation round-trip for a file that essentially never
# changes.
/og-image.png
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=86400

# --- Tier 3: unhashed files that DO change on ordinary content passes ------
# Site/state/boundary GeoJSON and the MapLibre style JSON are the two
# things this repo actually regenerates during normal work (this session's
# boundary-simplification pass being a direct example). max-age keeps
# same-day repeat plays instant; stale-while-revalidate bounds the worst
# case to "serve the slightly-stale copy once more while fetching the new
# one in the background" rather than "pinned to the old file for a full
# day" -- a returning player self-heals on their very next visit after a
# redeploy, no manual cache purge required.
#
# Content-Type is set explicitly here (not just Cache-Control) because
# Cloudflare only auto-compresses (gzip/brotli) a fixed allowlist of
# response content types, and .geojson/.topojson are non-standard
# extensions that its default static-asset MIME lookup does not reliably
# map into that allowlist -- confirmed via a throttled-network capture
# where these files transferred at their full raw size (e.g. 423 KB for
# india-boundary.geojson) instead of their ~128 KB gzip size. Explicitly
# setting a compressible Content-Type (application/geo+json is on
# Cloudflare's compression allowlist; .topojson has no registered MIME
# type of its own, so application/json is the correct compressible
# fallback) fixes this regardless of what the default guess was.
#
# india-states.geojson itself has no rule here (or any presence in public/
# at all) -- the client only ever fetches the derived .topojson below (see
# useMapState.js + scripts/convertStatesTopo.js); the raw geojson is
# build-source-only now, kept at scripts/source-data/india-states.geojson.
/india-states.topojson
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

/india-boundary.geojson
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/geo+json; charset=utf-8

/india-state-labels.geojson
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/geo+json; charset=utf-8

/physical-features.geojson
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/geo+json; charset=utf-8

# Site metadata (name/category/state/centroid per protected area) -- fetched
# by App.jsx at runtime, not bundled as a JS module (see that file's
# loadSites() comment: native JSON.parse on a plain fetch response beats
# parsing the same data through the general JS grammar as an import()'d
# module). Same regenerate-on-a-content-pass profile as the GeoJSON files
# above, so it gets the same cache tier.
/protected-areas.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8

/map-style.json
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400

# Per-site boundary polygons (BottomCard's "Site Boundary" toggle) -- same
# tier as above, same reasoning, just a directory of 837 files instead of
# a handful of named ones. Individually small enough that compression
# matters less per-file, but it's free and correct to set regardless.
/boundaries/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=3600, stale-while-revalidate=86400
  Content-Type: application/geo+json; charset=utf-8
